        "section": "limits",
        "source_id": "sqlite-3.53.4-docs",
        "start": 0,
        "text": "A host parameter is a place-holder in an SQL statement that is filled in using one of the sqlite3_bind_XXXX() interfaces. Many SQL programmers are familiar with using a question mark (\"?\") as a host parameter. SQLite also supports named host parameters prefaced by \":\", \"$\", or \"@\" and numbered host parameters of the form \"?123\".",
        "title": "limits"
      },
      {
        "end": 615,
        "estimated_tokens": 66,
        "kind": "code_block",
        "page_id": 12,
        "position": 2,
        "record_id": "bfdc468a8ef07d73294704ca",
        "score": 0.3943047925021965,
        "section": "lang expr",
        "source_id": "sqlite-3.53.4-docs",
        "start": 411,
        "text": "A \"variable\" or \"parameter\" token specifies a placeholder in the expression for a value that is filled in at runtime using the sqlite3_bind() family of C/C++ interfaces. Parameters can take several forms:",
        "title": "lang expr"
      },
      {
        "end": 586,
        "estimated_tokens": 77,
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "page_id": 12,
        "position": 1,
        "record_id": "a4cfdfd3ccd30e1c9f741e15",
        "score": 0.3774585360461514,
        "section": "limits",
        "source_id": "sqlite-3.53.4-docs",
        "start": 332,
        "text": "Each host parameter in an SQLite statement is assigned a number. The numbers normally begin with 1 and increase by one with each new parameter. However, when the \"?123\" form is used, the host parameter number is the number that follows the question mark.",
        "title": "limits"
      },
      {
        "end": 755,
        "estimated_tokens": 50,
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "page_id": 12,
        "position": 3,
        "record_id": "bfdc468a8ef07d73294704ca",
        "score": 0.3722214591688631,
        "section": "lang expr",
        "source_id": "sqlite-3.53.4-docs",
        "start": 617,
        "text": "?NNN A question mark followed by a number NNN holds a spot for the NNN-th parameter. NNN must be between 1 and SQLITE_MAX_VARIABLE_NUMBER.",
        "title": "lang expr"
      },
      {
        "end": 1319,
        "estimated_tokens": 77,
        "kind": "code_block",
        "page_id": 4,
